From: Akisaira <akisaira@outlook.com>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: sc8280xp-huawei-gaokun3: throttle on the
 board thermistors

Several of the PMIC thermistors on this board sit close to the SoC, and
the Windows firmware throttles on four of them. Huawei's PEP driver
configuration maps SYS_THERM1-4 to the ACPI thermal zones QCOM065D,
QCOM065E, QCOM065F and QCOM0660. The DSDT shipped with the machine
enables five passive zones on them. Linux throttles only at the 85 degC
die trips. After 100 s of full GPU load, sys_therm1 reads 61 degC (the
first Windows zone trips at 54 degC) and sys_therm7 reads 80 degC, while
no cooling device has engaged.

Describe the Windows zones with the DSDT's initial values:

  sensor      passive  throttles     minimum performance (_MTL)
  sys_therm1  54 degC  CPUs and GPU  60 %
  sys_therm2  58 degC  CPUs and GPU  56 %
  sys_therm3  58 degC  GPU           60 %
  sys_therm3  65 degC  CPUs and GPU  none
  sys_therm4  64 degC  CPUs and GPU  40 %

Windows also dims the backlight in the last four zones. That is not
mirrored here. Huawei's SSDT rewrites these trip temperatures at runtime,
from an EC event and from its performance-mode WMI method; in
performance mode, for example, sys_therm1-3 trip at 60/64/68 degC. The
kernel keeps the boot defaults, and userspace can change the trip
temperatures through sysfs.

Each zone gets the 100 degC critical trip Windows uses (_CRT 99.85 degC).
Windows never throttles below the _MTL share of full performance. Map
that to the deepest cooling state whose frequency is still at or above
that share of the maximum. On the 2.69 GHz part this gives:

  - little cluster: 1555.2, 1440.0 or 1017.6 MHz
  - big cluster: 1632.0, 1516.8 or 1171.2 MHz
  - GPU: 500 or 410 MHz

The big-cluster OPP table in sc8280xp.dtsi goes up to 2995.2 MHz. If a
3.0 GHz part exposes those three extra states, the same caps give 66, 62
and 51 % instead of 60, 56 and 40 %.

The ACPI zones sample every 5 s, and every 20 s for the GPU-only zone.
Poll every second instead while passive cooling is active. Under full
GPU load, sys_therm1 still rises by about 0.2 degC/s when it crosses
54 degC. The ADC-TM threshold interrupt reports the first crossing.

No Windows zone on this machine uses sys_therm7. It rises with GPU load
and drops by 20 degC within seconds of the load stopping, so it probably
sits next to a power component. Add a backstop at 95 degC. The value is
the passive trip of the reference design's zone on that thermistor; on
this machine that zone is disabled.

Drop skin-temp-thermal. It reads sys_therm6, which no Windows zone here
uses, and its trips are the ThinkPad X13s values: 55/58 degC passive on
the big cores and a 73 degC critical trip. It has never been registered
because QCOM_SPMI_ADC_TM5 was a module that nothing loads. Enabling that
driver would make the zone live, including its critical trip.

With these zones, the same GPU load starts throttling after about 40 s.
sys_therm1 then settles near 58 degC and sys_therm7 near 62 degC, and the
GPU runs at 500 MHz with 80 % of its initial throughput.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Origin: local
Upstream-Status: Pending
